  "sources": [
    "https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/IDEs/Conceptual/xcode_help-command_shortcuts/Introduction/Introduction.html",
    "https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/IDEs/Conceptual/xcode_help-command_shortcuts/MenuCommands/MenuCommands014.html",
    "https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/IDEs/Conceptual/xcode_help-command_shortcuts/TextCmdsByType/TextCmdsByType.html",
    "https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/IDEs/Conceptual/xcode_help-command_shortcuts/SystemAndOther/SystemAndOther.html"
  ],
  "note": "Mac only. From Apple's \"Xcode Keyboard Shortcuts and Gestures\" reference: the \"Menu Command Shortcuts (By Menu)\" tables (Xcode, File, Edit, View, Navigate, Editor, Product, Window and Help menus) and the \"Text Commands (By Type)\" tables. A menu name is not a mode, so menu commands carry an empty context - except where Apple's own tables add a Context column, whose values are kept as the context (\"Source editor\", \"Interface Builder\", \"Data model\", \"PDF\", \"Scripting definition\", \"Source code\", \"Hex editor\"), and the Product menu's Debug submenu, kept as \"Debug\". Apple groups the text commands by command type, which is a grouping rather than a mode, so they are filed under \"Source editor\", Apple's own name for where they apply. This is the only complete shortcut list Apple publishes; it now lives in the Apple developer library archive and describes Xcode 4, so a few bindings have moved in later releases - Xcode > Settings > Key Bindings shows the current ones. Commands Apple lists with no key binding are left out. Also omitted: the three-finger swipe, two-finger tap and two-finger swipe gestures from the introduction, which are not keyboard chords; the \"Delete\" text command, whose only binding is the Clear Line key; and the \"Other System and Application Shortcuts\" page, which lists macOS-wide shortcuts by description rather than Xcode commands - see the macOS entry for those. Shifted punctuation Apple prints as a single glyph is written as Shift plus the unshifted key on a US layout, so Command-} becomes Cmd+Shift+].",
